# {{ .name }}

.NET streaming extractor integration. One run = one batch: the runner detects
a complete batch of files on the source, streams each file record-by-record
in constant memory through its registered streamed extractor, publishes one
summary CloudEvent to the `{{ .topic }}` topic on the Dapr pub/sub, archives
the consumed inputs, and exits. Scheduling lives outside the block —
activation cadence is deployment configuration (a Kubernetes CronJob in
production); locally the system host runs the block once at startup.

## How a run flows

```
scheduled run (CronJob / host)
   │
   ▼
detect batch (incoming prefix + ready marker)
   │  nothing ready ──▶ quiet exit 0
   ▼
per file in the batch:
   │
   ▼
stream records ──▶ validate + transform ──▶ JSONL output
   │
   ▼
serialize batch summary (per-file record/reject counts)
   │
   ▼
publish one CloudEvent to '{{ .topic }}' (subject = batch id)
   │
   ▼
archive consumed inputs (incoming/ → processed/)
   │
   ▼
exit 0
```

Off the happy path: a ready-but-incomplete batch routes a business incident
(and stays in place), a technical failure (dead source, dead broker) fails
the run with exit 1 — the CronJob records it — and the batch is re-detected
on the next run. Batch idempotency is the detect step's contract: an
archived batch is invisible to detection, so a completed batch never
re-runs.

`EventSource`/`EventType` in `src/Constants.cs` come from the scaffold
parameters `eventSource`/`eventType`; unset, they derive as
`urn:<organization>:<app-id>` and
`<organization-lower>.<first-topic-segment>.<last-topic-segment>`. Set them
when migrating an existing component so consumers keep seeing the same
envelope.

## Configuration

Settings live in `src/appsettings.json`, overridable per environment via
`appsettings.{Environment}.json` and environment variables; deployment
overlays the file via ConfigMap. `IConfiguration` is in DI. Batch layout
(prefixes + ready-marker name) binds to `src/Configuration/IngestOptions.cs`
from the `Ingest` section.

## Build and test

```bash
task build
task test
```

The extractor does not run standalone. It runs via its system host, which
runs it once at startup, provides the source binding, pub/sub, and platform
services, and carries the sample data used to seed a run. A re-run is a host
restart. `task test` covers the component-level loop: batch-run integration
tests plus the composition smoke test, all without a sidecar
(`task test:integration`). `task coverage` runs the integration suite with
branch coverage (Cobertura report under the integration project's
`TestResults/`).

## Testing strategy

The integration suite runs against `Intropy.Framework.Testing` fakes — no
Dapr sidecar, no Testcontainers, no live platform services. The one
principle throughout: **build the pipeline exactly as production composition
does, fake only the edges, assert on fake state.**

### Layers

| Layer | Project / files | What it pins |
|---|---|---|
| Batch run | `test/{{ .name }}.Test.Integration/BatchRunTests.cs` | The full batch chain (detect → sub-pipelines → serialize → publish → archive) over one detected batch, with `FakeTopic` at the publish edge and an in-memory source adapter. Pins the batch consumption contract: a complete batch publishes one summary event and is archived; nothing-ready is a quiet clean run; a publish failure leaves the batch in place. |
| Composition | `test/{{ .name }}.Test.Integration/CompositionTests.cs` | The real DI graph from `Composition` builds without a sidecar and the keyed source adapter resolves. |

### The edges and their fakes

- **Source files** — `InMemoryFileAdapter` (implements the streaming
  `IFileStreamAdapter` contract: commit-on-dispose writes, read-after-N-bytes
  fault injection). Seeded with batch folders; asserts read file state
  directly (`Files`).
- **Topic** — `FakeTopic<TCtx>`, swapped into DI in place of the production
  `SendStep<TCtx>` registration (a `DaprTopicPublisher`), exactly like the
  other externals. `Events` exposes exactly what the real publisher would
  have encoded; `SendException` simulates a dead broker.
- **Platform services** — `FakeIdempotencyServiceClient` and
  `FakeBusinessIncidentServiceClient`, swapped the same way.

### Behaviors pinned

- **One summary event per batch.** Per-entity record/reject counts come from
  the streamed contexts the sub-pipeline step accumulates; the event subject
  is the batch id.
- **Archive is the idempotency mechanism.** Consumed inputs move to the
  processed prefix only after a successful publish; detection never sees an
  archived batch.
- **Nothing ready is clean.** A run with no ready marker completes quietly
  (exit 0) without publishing.

## Shared contracts

The canonical contract types (the `{{ .contract }}` batch summary with its
nested per-file record) live in the sibling `../{{ .contracts }}` project,
referenced from `src/{{ .name }}.csproj`. It was scaffolded together with
this project (or already existed — components in the same parent directory
share it); the consuming component deserializes exactly those records.

## Container image

```bash
docker build --build-context contracts=../{{ .contracts }} -t {{ .appId }} .
```

Multi-stage build on the .NET 10 chiseled runtime. The shared contracts
sibling enters the build as the `contracts` named context (it lies outside
this build context). The image contains only the app — the Dapr sidecar and
components come from the deployment environment.

## Pipeline

One batch per run (`src/Batch/`, hosted by the framework's
`RunToCompletionRunner`, which owns the sidecar lifecycle, tracing, and the
0/1/2 exit-code mapping via `src/Batch/BatchJob.cs`):

```text
// Batch pipeline transitions:
//
//         ┌─────────────────────────────┐
//         │ 1. DetectStep               ├──── nothing ready ─► [[Cancelled]] (quiet exit)
//         └──────────────┬──────────────┘
//                        │ oldest ready batch (ready marker present)
//                        ▼
//         ┌─────────────────────────────┐
//         │ 2. SubPipelineStep          │  per file: streamed extractor matched
//         └──────────────┬──────────────┘  by entity type; constant-memory
//                        │                │  validate+transform → JSONL output
//                        ▼
//         ┌─────────────────────────────┐
//         │ 3. SerializeSummaryStep     │  one summary CloudEvent
//         └──────────────┬──────────────┘  (subject = batch id)
//                        ▼
//         ┌─────────────────────────────┐
//         │ 4. Dapr topic publisher     │
//         └──────────────┬──────────────┘
//                        ▼
//         ┌─────────────────────────────┐
//         │ 5. ArchiveStep              │  incoming/ → processed/
//         └─────────────────────────────┘
//
// A business failure (e.g. ready-but-incomplete batch) routes an incident;
// technical failures fail the run and leave the batch for the next one.
```
{{- if .empty }}

Every detection/serialize/streamed step body throws `NotImplementedException`
— this project was scaffolded with empty bodies for a migration agent to fill
in. The batch chain wiring (sub-pipeline resolution, sender, archive,
business-incident routing) is already in place; define the producer's
delivery format in `DetectStep`, the summary shape in `SerializeSummaryStep`,
and the per-entity record logic in `src/Process/Streamed/`.

The batch-run tests fail from the start by design (the throwing steps route
the run to a technical/business failure without publishing). The suite flips
green piece by piece as the steps are implemented — the failing tests are the
to-do list.
{{- else }}

1. `DetectStep` scans the incoming prefix for ready markers, picks the oldest
   ready batch, and stamps the batch id into the context.
2. `SubPipelineStep` matches each batch file to its registered streamed
   extractor by entity type and streams it (constant memory) to the output
   prefix as JSONL, accumulating per-file counts on the context.
3. `SerializeSummaryStep` builds the one summary CloudEvent from the batch +
   accumulated counts; subject = batch id.
4. The Dapr topic publisher sends it to `pubsub` / `{{ .topic }}`.
5. `ArchiveStep` moves the consumed inputs and the ready marker to the
   processed prefix — which is also what hides the batch from detection on
   later runs.
{{- end }}
